Kadane Pattern Recognition

This is the format we'll use for every pattern.

Pattern Name
Kadane's Algorithm
Core Idea

At every index, decide:

Continue the current subarray.
OR start a new subarray.

Choose whichever gives the larger sum.

🎯 Recognition Keywords

If you see these words, Kadane should come to your mind.

Green Signals (Strong Indicators)

✅ Maximum subarray sum

✅ Largest contiguous sum

✅ Best contiguous segment

✅ Continuous subarray

✅ Maximum profit from consecutive days (after converting to differences)

✅ Highest scoring streak

✅ Maximum gain in a continuous interval

Yellow Signals (Think Carefully)

These may still be Kadane, but verify first.

🟡 Minimum subarray sum

🟡 Maximum circular subarray

🟡 Maximum alternating sum

🟡 Maximum difference using contiguous elements

Red Signals (Usually NOT Kadane)

❌ Fixed-size window

Example:

Maximum sum of exactly k elements

👉 Sliding Window

❌ Target sum

Example:

Longest subarray with sum = k

👉 Prefix Sum + HashMap

❌ Product instead of sum

Example:

Maximum Product Subarray

👉 Different DP approach (because multiplying by a negative can flip the answer)

❌ Non-contiguous subsequence

Example:

Maximum subsequence sum

👉 Not Kadane

Subsequence ≠ Subarray.

✅ When to Use Kadane

Use Kadane when all of these are true:

✔️ Array

✔️ Contiguous (Subarray)

✔️ Need maximum/minimum sum

✔️ Can choose any length (not fixed)

✔️ One pass optimisation is expected

❌ When NOT to Use Kadane

Don't use Kadane if the problem asks for:

Exactly k elements
Longest subarray with a target sum
Count of subarrays
Product instead of sum
Non-contiguous subsequences
Arbitrary start/end constraints that change the recurrence
Mental Decision Tree (Interview Shortcut)

When you read a problem, ask these questions in order:

Is it an ARRAY?
        │
        ▼
Is it asking about a CONTIGUOUS subarray?
        │
        ▼
Is it asking for MAXIMUM or MINIMUM SUM?
        │
        ▼
Is the subarray allowed to be ANY LENGTH?
        │
        ▼
YES → Think Kadane

If you answer No at any step, pause and think about another pattern.

🧠 English Algorithm

Don't memorise code.

Memorise this:

For every element:

1. Decide:
   Continue the previous subarray
   OR
   Start a new subarray.

2. Keep the better choice.

3. Update the best answer seen so far.
Complexity

Time:

O(n)

Space:

O(1)
